![medium](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/medium-priority.svg)

The `mcp.requiresBinary` value is directly interpolated into a command string for `run()` (which uses `execSync`). While the values are currently hardcoded and safe, this is a dangerous pattern that could lead to command injection if the MCP registry data were ever sourced from a less trusted location. It's a good practice to validate inputs before using them in shell commands.

```javascript
    if (mcp.requiresBinary) {
      if (!/^[a-zA-Z0-9._-]+$/.test(mcp.requiresBinary)) {
        console.error(`[aidevops] Invalid characters in requiresBinary for MCP '${mcp.name}': ${mcp.requiresBinary}`);
        continue;
      }
      const binaryPath = run(`which ${mcp.requiresBinary}`);
      if (!binaryPath) {
        // Disable tools if binary not available
        if (mcp.toolPattern) {
          config.tools[mcp.toolPattern] = false;
        }
        continue;
      }
    }
```

_⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_

**Disable already-registered MCPs when required binaries are missing.**

Right now you skip registration, but if the MCP already exists in config it can still be enabled and fail at runtime. This undermines the “skip if binary missing” behavior.


<details>
<summary>🛠️ Proposed fix</summary>

```diff
     if (mcp.requiresBinary) {
       const binaryPath = run(`which ${mcp.requiresBinary}`);
       if (!binaryPath) {
         // Disable tools if binary not available
         if (mcp.toolPattern) {
           config.tools[mcp.toolPattern] = false;
         }
+        // Ensure previously-registered MCPs are disabled too
+        if (config.mcp[mcp.name]) {
+          config.mcp[mcp.name].enabled = false;
+        }
         continue;
       }
     }
```
</details>

